  situation: 'Djibrilla sees that the coast of West Africa and the coast of Brazil fit like two pieces of a puzzle. His teacher says that a German scientist had the same idea in 1912.',
  sitQA: [['What is the problem in the situation?', 'Djibrilla wonders if the continents were once joined.'], ['Who was the scientist?', 'Alfred Wegener.'], ['What evidence can support the idea?', 'The fit of the coasts, fossils and rocks.']],
  justification: 'This lesson helps us to understand how scientists first explained the movement of continents.',
  activities: [
    { sec: 'I. ISOSTASY', img: 'isostasy.gif', caption: 'Animation: the crust sinks under ice and rises when it melts.', q: 'Why does the crust rise again when the ice melts?', a: 'The crust floats on the mantle; when the weight is removed, it rises (isostatic recovery).', noProj: 'put a piece of wood in water and press it down, then release it.' },
    { sec: 'II. CONTINENTAL DRIFT', img: 'jigsaw.gif', caption: 'Animation: closing the Atlantic Ocean.', q: 'What does the fit of Africa and South America suggest?', a: 'They were once joined in one landmass (Pangaea).', noProj: 'cut out the two continents from a paper map.' },
    { sec: 'III. EVIDENCE', img: 'f4_fossil.jpg', caption: 'A fossil in rock.', q: 'The same fossils (Mesosaurus, Glossopteris) are found in Brazil and southern Africa. What does this prove?', a: 'The continents were joined when these plants and animals lived.', noProj: 'explain that the animal could not swim across an ocean.' },
    { sec: 'III. EVIDENCE', img: 'lsa_striation.jpg', caption: 'Scratches left on rock by an old glacier (ice sheet).', q: 'Marks of old ice sheets are found in India and Africa. What does this show?', a: 'These lands were once near the South Pole and have drifted.', noProj: 'describe marks left by glaciers on rocks.' },
    { sec: 'IV. STRENGTHS AND WEAKNESSES', img: 'plates_big.png', caption: 'The plates of the Earth today.', q: 'Wegener could not explain what moves the continents. Which later theory explained it?', a: 'The theory of plate tectonics (mantle convection currents).', noProj: 'draw the plates on a world sketch.' },
  ],
  summary: [
    { title: 'I. Definitions', items: [['Isostasy:', 'It is the balance of the crust floating on the denser mantle.'], ['Continental drift:', 'It is the slow movement of the continents across the Earth.'], ['Pangaea:', 'It is the single supercontinent that existed about 250 million years ago.']] },
    { title: 'II. Isostasy', items: [['A. Meaning:', 'Blocks of crust float on the mantle like wood on water. Thick, light crust stands high and thin, dense crust stands low.'], ['B. Isostatic recovery:', 'When a load of ice melts, the crust rises slowly. This happens today in Scandinavia and Canada.']] },
    { title: 'III. Continental Drift (Wegener, 1912)', items: [['A. The idea:', 'All continents were joined in Pangaea. It split into Laurasia in the north and Gondwanaland in the south, which then broke up.'], ['B. Evidence:', 'Coasts fit together, and the same fossils and rocks are found on both sides of the Atlantic. Marks of old ice are found in hot lands.'], ['C. Strengths:', 'It explained the fit of the continents and the distribution of fossils. It prepared the theory of plate tectonics.'], ['D. Weaknesses:', 'Wegener could not explain the force that moves the continents. Many scientists rejected his idea at first.']] },
  ],
  evaluation: [['What is isostasy?', 'The balance of the crust floating on the denser mantle.'], ['Give two pieces of evidence for continental drift.', 'The jigsaw fit of the coasts and the same fossils on both sides of the Atlantic.']],
